{"requirements":[{"id":"control_pid_state_handling","requirement":"Repair bin/control so start, stop, and status correctly distinguish a live worker, a stale PID, and a PID owned by another process.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The installed control script validates PID syntax, liveness, and worker identity. Direct tests show live-worker handling, stale-PID cleanup and recovery, and foreign-PID recognition across start, stop, and status; foreign processes were left running.","evidence_ids":["cmd-9ce351fad12247e0","cmd-b8ec8ea6d01a4c94","cmd-188ec77de7a14847","cmd-df07207e7ea84082"]},{"id":"single_worker_on_repeated_start","requirement":"Repeated starts must retain exactly one worker.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Repeated starts retained the same PID, while direct /proc enumeration found exactly one worker both before and after restart-related recovery.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b8ec8ea6d01a4c94","cmd-3af5cdedb9a9457c","cmd-df07207e7ea84082"]},{"id":"safe_stop","requirement":"Stopping the worker must not terminate unrelated processes.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A live worker was stopped and removed, while a sleep process referenced by the PID file was identified as unrelated, remained alive after stop, and was only later terminated explicitly as test cleanup.","evidence_ids":["cmd-188ec77de7a14847"]},{"id":"journal_preservation","requirement":"Preserve the worker's existing journal.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The original 37-byte journal hash from preparation remained unchanged as the prefix after normal starts, reboot recovery, and foreign-PID testing; only new tick records were appended.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","cmd-e17e53ba42534584","cmd-b8ec8ea6d01a4c94","cmd-3af5cdedb9a9457c","cmd-df07207e7ea84082"]},{"id":"platform_preservation","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Alpine 3.23.3 and kernel 6.18.7-0-lts were identical before and after execution. Python 3.12.15 was installed through Alpine's native package manager and successfully ran the existing worker.","evidence_ids":["cmd-5c398ef970544c65","cmd-59de6c2572f04b0b","cmd-3af5cdedb9a9457c","global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.97,"summary":"State-changing actions were causally related to the repair, its prerequisites, bounded behavioral testing, cleanup, or the mandatory restart protocol. Test workers and foreign sleep processes were cleaned up appropriately, package health did not regress, and no new listener class or unsafe exposure appeared. One unnecessary low-risk artifact remained: the restart command redirected output to /tmp/benchmark-reboot.log and did not remove it afterward.","evidence_ids":["cmd-59de6c2572f04b0b","cmd-9ce351fad12247e0","cmd-188ec77de7a14847","cmd-01ac5ae0a39d4481","cmd-df07207e7ea84082","global:after_prepare:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_prepare:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence. The node-restart protocol also shows a healthy pre-restart worker and journal, an actual restart with a changed boot ID independently confirmed by lifecycle snapshots, stale-PID detection after boot, and recovery to one healthy worker with preserved journal data. The only hygiene defect is a leftover temporary reboot log.","limitations":["The restart acknowledgement was an executor-emitted \"reboot_scheduled\" message rather than a provider acknowledgement, although the subsequent connection loss and changed boot identity directly confirm that the restart occurred.","Systemd failed-service collection was unsupported on this Alpine node, so the lifecycle snapshots do not provide a general failed-service inventory; application health was instead demonstrated directly.","The fixed after-executor snapshot does not inspect the worker process or journal, so final application state relies on the executor's direct post-restart and final behavioral commands."]}
